<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love These Entertainers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify messages for peers. Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify messages for peers by using gestures and actions to convey meaning (e.g., says, "He's a singer." and pretends to hold a microphone and sing to represent the singer Sech). Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions with simple gestures (e.g., mimics jumping moves and says "Irving Saladino was a long jumper."). Can use visual aids to simplify achievements of a famous Panamanian (e.g., points to medals and says, "He won many awards.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Guess the Famous Panamanian" game. The teacher describes an entertainer or athlete using simple words and gestures (e.g., "This person is a singer. He is very popular. He performs on big stages." *Teacher mimes holding a microphone and singing.* "Who is he?"). Students guess the person (e.g., Sech). The teacher uses a few other examples like Irving Saladino ("He was an athlete. He competed in jumping." *Teacher mimes jumping.*) to activate prior knowledge of vocabulary (singer, athlete, perform, compete, popular, famous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify information and use gestures. The teacher shows a picture of Roberto Durán and says, "Roberto Durán is a boxer. He is the best boxer in Panama." Then the teacher says, "If someone doesn't understand 'boxer,' I can say 'He fights in a ring!' *Teacher makes a punching motion.* or 'He wins many awards for boxing!' *Teacher points to an imaginary medal.*" The teacher demonstrates simplifying "greatest" to "the best in the world" to clarify understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't understand a word, what can you do? (Simplify, use gestures, explain simply.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to make things clear for others? (So everyone can understand, so we can work together.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Famous Panamanians".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents short profiles (text and images) of two famous Panamanians: Atheyna Bylon (boxer) and Sech (singer). The profiles include a mix of past achievements (past simple) and ongoing activities (present continuous for future events) and descriptive adjectives. The teacher projects an example of how their "Famous Panamanians Project" poster/webpage could look, with brief, well-structured information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Profile 1 (Atheyna Bylon):</b> "Atheyna Bylon is a famous athlete. She is a specialized boxer. She won a medal in the Olympic Games in 2021. Currently, she is training for a new tournament. She inspires many young women in Panama."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Profile 2 (Sech):</b> "Sech is a popular singer. He is more popular than many other singers. He achieved global recognition with his music. He performed in many concerts last year. Next month, he is releasing a new song."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer gist questions about the profiles:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is Atheyna Bylon? What did she achieve?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Sech doing next month?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which person is an athlete? Which is an entertainer?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Sech more popular than Atheyna Bylon? (No, they are different types of famous people)."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher guides students to notice -ing endings for ongoing actions and -ed for past achievements, practicing pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present continuous for future events, Comparatives and superlatives, Past simple for achievements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, singer, actor, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan, win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete, famous, popular, talented, skilled, specialized, more, best.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronunciation of -ing verb endings and -ed in past tense (e.g., "watching," "performed"). Practice reading inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) in sentences (e.g., "He improved his own marks.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing achievements, Comparing figures.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Simplify & Match (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with complex sentences about various famous Panamanians on one side (e.g., "Mariano Rivera is widely recognized as an unparalleled relief pitcher in baseball history.") and simplified versions with gestures described on the other side (e.g., "Mariano Rivera won many awards. *Teacher points to medals.* He's a very good baseball player."). Students match the complex sentence with its simplified counterpart. Teacher emphasizes using target vocabulary and checking pronunciation of -ed endings in words like "recognized" and "achieved."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Rephrase for Clarity (Semi-Controlled):</b> In pairs, students receive cards with facts about famous Panamanians. One student reads a fact (e.g., "Sech is releasing a new song in the future."). The other student must rephrase it simply and use a gesture or action to help explain (e.g., "Sech is making new music! *Teacher mimes singing and writing.*"). They practice using present continuous for future events and past simple for achievements. Teacher monitors for correct use of comparatives (e.g., "He is more talented than some other musicians.") and pronunciation of -ing verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Clarify it! (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in groups of three. Student A describes a famous Panamanian's achievement or ongoing activity (e.g., "Irving Saladino was a long jumper."). Student B pretends not to fully understand or asks a clarifying question (e.g., "A long jumper? What's that?"). Student C (the mediator) must clarify the misunderstanding using gestures, simpler words, or visual aids if available (e.g., Student C mimics jumping moves and says, "He jumps very far! He competed in track and field and won the best award."). Students rotate roles, ensuring everyone practices mediating information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Famous Panamanians".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will present their "Famous Panamanians Project" (poster or web page) in small groups. Each student, or pair if working collaboratively, takes turns presenting their chosen famous Panamanian to their group members. The core task is to mediate the information effectively:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Presenting & Explaining:</b> Students describe their famous Panamanian, highlighting their achievements (past simple) and ongoing activities (present continuous for future events), and comparing them to others (comparatives/superlatives). They must use their poster/web page as a visual aid.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediating & Clarifying:</b> Group members are encouraged to ask questions or express confusion (e.g., "What does 'achieve global recognition' mean?"). The presenting student must then simplify their message, use gestures, point to parts of their visual aid, or rephrase to clarify any misunderstandings (e.g., for "Sech achieved global recognition," they might say, "He is very famous all over the world! People everywhere know his music." and make a gesture of wide arms for 'world').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Focus:</b> Teacher reminds students to be mindful of clear pronunciation for -ing and -ed endings.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Peer feedback and teacher observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> After each group presentation, group members use a simple peer assessment checklist to provide feedback on how well their classmate mediated the information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Peer Assessment Checklist (Yes/No/Needs more practice):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the presenter explain the famous Panamanian clearly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they use simple words when needed?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they use gestures or point to their poster/web page to help explain?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they use words like 'entertainer,' 'athlete,' 'achievement,' 'awards,' 'won,' 'trained,' 'famous,' 'more popular,' 'best'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was their pronunciation of -ing and -ed words generally clear?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> Intelligible pronunciation (especially -ing verb endings and -ed in past tense), use of target vocabulary (entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, etc.), clarity of simplified messages, effective use of gestures/visual aids, collaborative interaction in clarifying. Teacher notes general class performance for future planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I did well today to help my classmates understand was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time, I want to try to improve my mediation by..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using gestures and simple words help you explain things better today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of clarifying information for your peers was challenging, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to simplify messages for peers using gestures, especially when referring to occupations (singer, boxer) or simple achievements (won awards). The use of the posters/web pages significantly aided visual mediation. Some students struggled with rephrasing more abstract concepts like "achieved global recognition," needing more explicit modeling. Adjustments needed for the next unit could include more varied scenarios for mediation, perhaps involving problem-solving or explaining instructions, to develop deeper clarification skills. I will focus on providing sentence frames for complex concepts to simplify, and encourage more diverse gestures beyond simple mimes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Love These Entertainers" theme and the "Famous Panamanians Project." The mediation skills practiced today will be valuable in any future collaborative project or presentation where students need to explain their ideas clearly and effectively to an audience, applying their language skills in a practical way.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>